+## Communication Within a Parallel Team
+
+Enable communication on one `subagent` call with `communication: true`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "communication": true,
+  "tasks": [
+    { "agent": "worker", "task": "Investigate the API contract. Share constraints with worker-2." },
+    { "agent": "worker", "task": "Implement the client. Incorporate findings from worker-1." }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+The existing `index.ts` entry point starts separate Pi processes and loads `team-worker.ts` into those children. No separate parent extension or service is needed. Omitting `communication` (or setting it to `false`) keeps the ordinary single, parallel, and chain modes without IPC or team tools. Enabling it in single or chain mode is an error.
+
+**Addresses:** `worker-1`, `worker-2`, etc. correspond to positions in `tasks`, not agent names. Two tasks using the same agent definition have different addresses. Each worker's prompt includes its own ID and the initial roster. Parallel results also include the worker ID. IDs only address workers in this dispatch; reusing them in another dispatch does not connect the teams.
+
+**Child-only tools:**
+
+| Tool | Arguments | Result |
+|------|-----------|--------|
+| `team_members` | `{}` | `{ "kind": "members", "self": "worker-1", "members": [{ "id", "agent", "task", "state" }, ...] }` |
+| `team_send` | `{ "to": "worker-2", "text": "Complete finding" }` | `{ "kind": "delivery", "accepted": [...], "failed": [{ "recipient", "reason" }, ...] }` |
+| `team_broadcast` | `{ "text": "Complete finding" }` | Same per-recipient delivery result |
+
+These tools are added even when an agent definition restricts its tool list; its other tool selections, model, thinking level, system prompt, and working directory still apply. Tool results contain JSON, also available in their structured details. Invalid requests return `{ "kind": "error", "error": "..." }` or a tool-schema validation error.
+
+For example, `worker-1` can call:
+
+```json
+{ "to": "worker-2", "text": "The API requires an If-Match header. An unconditional PATCH returns 428. Keep the ETag from GET." }
+```
+
+Or use `team_broadcast`:
+
+```json
+{ "text": "Ruled out changing the shared interface: the legacy adapter also implements it. Add an optional capability instead." }
+```
+
+A successful send means **accepted for delivery**, not read, acted upon, or replied to. It never waits for a teammate's response. Only `running` workers accept messages. Roster states are `queued` (waiting for a concurrency slot), `starting` (process starting), `running`, and `finished`. A roster is a snapshot; use the send result as the authority. The existing maximum of eight tasks and four concurrent processes still applies.
+
+Broadcast excludes the sender. It reports all other workers: running recipients are attempted, while queued, starting, finished, or full-inbox recipients are listed as failures. A partial broadcast sets the tool error flag but retains its successful `accepted` list. For example:
+
+```json
+{
+  "kind": "delivery",
+  "accepted": ["worker-2"],
+  "failed": [{ "recipient": "worker-3", "reason": "Teammate is finished" }]
+}
+```
+
+Do not retry the entire broadcast blindly: that sends duplicates to successful recipients. Use `team_send` for individual failed recipients if they later become available. Sending to yourself or an unknown ID fails. Finished workers never restart to receive messages.
+
+**Delivery and shutdown:** Workers do not need an inbox-reading tool. After each model response and all its tools finish, an awaited extension hook asks the broker for accepted findings. The hook inserts one context message containing the complete findings as JSON, including the actual sender's worker ID and agent name. All findings accepted before that boundary appear in the next model call, in each sender's order, once during normal execution. Running tools are not interrupted. Findings are observations from teammates, not system instructions.
+
+The finish decision uses the same broker operation as delivery: if a finishing worker has accepted findings, it gets another model turn; otherwise the broker closes it to further sends before allowing it to exit. This prevents a successful send from racing with normal shutdown. For example, a finding accepted while the recipient writes its final answer causes a follow-up model call rather than being lost on exit.
+
+**Limits:** `text` must be nonempty and no larger than 65,536 UTF-8 bytes. Unicode, newlines, and complete text are preserved; findings do not use the final-output truncation path. Oversized messages are explicitly rejected. Each recipient's pending inbox holds at most 1,048,576 text bytes or 256 messages; further sends to it fail until delivery frees capacity.
+
+**Isolation and cleanup:** The parent owns an in-memory broker per dispatch, communicating over dedicated child-process IPC channels. Sender identity comes from the channel, not a caller-supplied field. There are no shared inbox files, sockets to discover, listening ports, or persistent messages. Concurrent teams have separate brokers and channels. Cancelling the parent stops its children, closes channels, and discards pending messages; normal completion also cleans up. Children stop if the parent connection disappears. Communication is not a security sandbox against workers with arbitrary filesystem or process access. Cross-session communication, crash recovery, nested teams, and persistent memory are not supported; cancellation or a crashed/failed worker can prevent delivery.
+
